ble now now  we can all get distracted by the food  fight in an impeachment or we can step  back and say what are the standards of a  presidency is this president exceeding  those standards falling below them and  what are the costs and we've never had a  chance to be able to do that have that  larger conversation about the presidency  with the most significant point of  debate between the Democratic  frontrunner of this point seems to be  over health care do you think that's  gonna be the deciding factor here in the  nominee it's a proxy debate in a way  which is to say whether Medicare for all  actually is ever gonna happen is not as  important is as I get a signal from a  candidate who's showing me their  commitment on that issue when Donald  Trump said the Mexicans are gonna pay  for the wall that was never gonna happen  his voters knew it was never gonna  happen but they thought that sends me a  signal about how committed he is to that  idea that's really what Medicare for all  is as a debate because think about what  a promo Kratt ik president will face  when they come into office let's imagine  they get elected they will have been  elected as a restoration of the American  presidency a Democrat that's going to be  a busy job there's going to be a lot to  do most of it oversees the political  landscape will have been beaten by a  year of politics getting  medicare-for-all passed through the  Senate or doing it even through  reconciliation or some you know clever  method is going to be extremely  difficult because they're gonna be so  busy doing  and politics will be a much even coarser  place than it is now so the conversation  they're having is really about a proxy  for things less about what actually is  gonna be able to happen with a Democrat  one fares this is gonna get a coarser  and more partisan before it gets less so  it's in everybody's interest to get it  course more course and to keep their  bases aligned at the moment John  Dickerson thank you very